 "question/problem" "value" "Simplify the expression $\\frac{2}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}^{12}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055661/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0b664fdfc17c5a0fb0cc22c02ac3b466d1e7a3a6eb1dd000f6857931357ba7d"} {"question" "q-104345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}^{12}}={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}^{-12}.\n$$\n\nDe Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-12$ and $\\phi =\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}^{12}} & =2{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}^{-12} \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-12\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-12\\theta )].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\phi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\phi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )$ for any input $\\phi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-12\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-12\\theta )]=2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\theta )].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055661/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0b664fdfc17c5a0fb0cc22c02ac3b466d1e7a3a6eb1dd000f6857931357ba7d"} {"question" "q-104345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/